<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Protest and freedom · 1876 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Vande Mataram</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="bn">বন্দে মাতরম্</bdi> / <bdi lang="bn-Latn">Bande Mataram</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Bengali poem from about 1876 that praises the motherland. It was a song of India's freedom movement and is now India's national song.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Bankim Chandra Chatterjee</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>many settings; the first tune by Jadunath Bhattacharya</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Bengalis; the Indian independence movement</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bengali, Sanskrit</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Chinsura (Chuchura), West Bengal, India</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>written in the 1870s, probably around 1876; first published 1882 in the novel Anandamath</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Bankim Chandra Chatterjee (written c. 1876, published 1882; he died in 1894))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a>&quot; is a poem written in Bengali, with many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> words, by Bankim Chandra Chatterjee, one of the first graduates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> University, who served the colonial government as a Deputy Collector and later a Deputy Magistrate. The idea is generally believed to have come to him around 1876, while he was still a government official; he wrote it at Chinsura (Chuchura), in a house of the Adhya family near the Hooghly river. At the time the administration was promoting &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot; as an anthem for its Indian subjects, which Indian nationalists disliked. The poem was published in 1882 in his novel Anandamath, set during the Sannyasi Rebellion of the previous century, and Jadunath Bhattacharya was asked to set a tune for it soon after it was written.</p><p>The song gained political weight when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a> sang it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> in 1896. Dakhina Charan Sen sang it at the Calcutta session of 1901, and the poet Sarala Devi Chaudurani at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a> session of 1905, the year the Congress adopted it as a pan-Indian salutation to the motherland. By then it was a marching song of political activists. The colonial government banned the novel and made singing the song in public a crime; many activists were imprisoned, and crowds sang it again and again in front of officials. Lala Lajpat Rai named a journal after it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, Hiralal Sen's 1905 political film ended with the chant, and on 20 May 1906 more than ten thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Muslims marched together under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a> flags in Barisal. In 1907 Bhikaiji Cama raised an early version of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s national flag in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> with the words written across its middle band. Matangini Hazra is said to have spoken them as her last words when she was shot by the police.</p><p>Later stanzas name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> goddesses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a> among them, and in 1937 this became a matter of dispute. Asked by Congress President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a>, Tagore replied that the core of the poem was a hymn to Durga, fitting in the novel but not in a parliament meant for all religious groups. On the recommendation of a committee including Maulana Azad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a>, Bose and Tagore, and with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> and Nehru, the Congress adopted only the first two stanzas in October 1937, and anyone who objected was left free to sing another song. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">All-India Muslim League</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a> opposed the song entirely that year. In January 1946 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>, Gandhi urged that the new greeting &quot;Jai Hind&quot; should not replace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a>.</p><p>The colonial ban was lifted at independence in 1947. On 24 January 1950 the Constituent Assembly, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-prasad">Rajendra Prasad</a> presiding, declared &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jana-gana-mana">Jana Gana Mana</a>&quot; the national anthem and ruled that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a>, for the part it had played in the struggle for freedom, should be honoured equally with it. The Constitution itself names no national song. Whether the poem first meant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> or all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> is debated: Sri Aurobindo called it the &quot;National Anthem of Bengal&quot;, while most parties and historians stress its pan-Indian role.</p><p>The disputes over its status continued. In July 2017 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> High Court ordered it sung weekly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>'s schools, then in November 2017 left the matter to the state government. In November 2022 the Union government told the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> High Court that the anthem and the national song stand on the same level. On 6 February 2026, for the poem's 150th anniversary, the Ministry of Home Affairs fixed an official rendition of 3 minutes 10 seconds and endorsed the full six stanzas at formal state events, and in May 2026 the Union Cabinet approved bringing the song under the Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act, 1971.</p><p>The poem has been set to more than a hundred tunes, many in classical ragas; the oldest surviving recordings date from 1907. Hemant Kumar's setting for the film Anand Math, sung by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lata-mangeshkar">Lata Mangeshkar</a>, came second in a BBC World Service poll of the world's best-known songs in 2002, and the All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Radio version, often credited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi-shankar">Ravi Shankar</a>, is in raga Desh.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of praise to the motherland, pictured as a mother. The first two stanzas evoke the beauty of the land without naming any deity. The later stanzas identify the mother with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> goddesses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a> among them. The mood is reverent and stirring.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Bengali</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Bengali</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">“বন্দে মাতরম্।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">“bande mātaram|</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“I bow to the Mother.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">সুজলাং সুফলাং</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">sujalāṁ suphalāṁ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rich in waters, rich in fruits,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">মলয়জশীতলাং</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">malaẏajaśītalāṁ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>cool with the wind from Malaya,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">শস্যশ্যামলাং</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">śasyaśyāmalāṁ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>dark green with crops,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">মাতরম্।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">mātaram|</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Mother.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">শুভ্র-জ্যোৎস্না-পুলকিত-যামিনীম্</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">śubhra-jyotsnā-pulakita-yāminīm</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Her nights thrilled by white moonlight,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ফুল্লকুসুমিত-দ্রুমদলশোভিনীম্,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">phullakusumita-drumadalaśobhinīm,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>beautiful with trees in full flower and leaf,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">সুহাসিনীং সুমধুরভাষিণীম্</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">suhāsinīṁ sumadhurabhāṣiṇīm</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>sweetly smiling, sweet of speech,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">সুখদাং বরদাং মাতরম্॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">sukhadāṁ baradāṁ mātaram||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>giver of joy, giver of boons, the Mother.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">সপ্তকোটীকণ্ঠকলকলনিনাদকরালে</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">saptakoṭīkaṇṭhakalakalaninādakarāle</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Terrible with the clamour of seventy million throats,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">দ্বিসপ্তকোটীভুজৈর্ধৃতখর-করবালে,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">dbisaptakoṭībhujairdhr̥takhara-karabāle,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with sharp swords held in twice seventy million arms,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">অবলা কেন মা এত বলে।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">abalā kena mā eta bale|</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>why, Mother, are you powerless, with so much strength?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">বহুবলধারিণীং</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">bahubaladhāriṇīṁ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Her who holds great strength,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">নমামি তারিণীং</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">namāmi tāriṇīṁ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bow to her who carries us across,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">রিপুদলবারিণীং</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">ripudalabāriṇīṁ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who drives back the hosts of the enemy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">মাতরম্।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">mātaram|</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Mother.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তুমি বিদ্যা তুমি ধর্ম</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tumi bidyā tumi dharma</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You are knowledge, you are dharma,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তুমি হৃদি তুমি মর্ম</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tumi hr̥di tumi marma</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you are the heart, you are the inmost core,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ত্বং হি প্রাণাঃ শরীরে।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tbaṁ hi prāṇāḥ śarīre|</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for you are the breaths of life in the body.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">বাহুতে তুমি মা শক্তি</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">bāhute tumi mā śakti</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the arm, Mother, you are strength,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">হৃদয়ে তুমি মা ভক্তি</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">hr̥daẏe tumi mā bhakti</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in the heart, Mother, you are devotion,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তোমারই প্রতিমা গড়ি</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tomāra:i pratimā gaṛi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it is your image we fashion</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">মন্দিরে মন্দিরে।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">mandire mandire|</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in temple after temple.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ত্বং হি দুর্গা দশপ্রহরণধারিণী</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tbaṁ hi durgā daśapraharaṇadhāriṇī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For you are Durgā, who bears the ten weapons,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">কমলা কমল-দলবিহারিণী</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">kamalā kamala-dalabihāriṇī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Kamalā, who wanders among the lotus petals,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">বাণী বিদ্যাদায়িনী</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">bāṇī bidyādāẏinī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Vāṇī, giver of knowledge;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">নমামি ত্বাং</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">namāmi tbāṁ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bow to you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">নমামি কমলাম্</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">namāmi kamalām</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bow to Kamalā,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">অমলাং অতুলাম্</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">amalāṁ atulām</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>spotless, peerless,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">সুজলাং সুফলাম্</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">sujalāṁ suphalām</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>rich in waters, rich in fruits,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">মাতরম্</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">mātaram</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Mother.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">বন্দে মাতরম্</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">bande mātaram</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bow to the Mother,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">শ্যামালাং সরলাং</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">śyāmālāṁ saralāṁ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>dark green, upright,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">সুস্মিতাং ভূষিতাম্</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">susmitāṁ bhūṣitām</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>sweetly smiling, adorned,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ধরনীং ভরণীম্</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">dharanīṁ bharaṇīm</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the earth, who sustains,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">মাতরম্।”</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">mātaram|”</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Mother.”</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay, Anandamath (1882), part 1, chapter 10. Text from <a href="https://bn.wikisource.org/wiki/%E0%A6%86%E0%A6%A8%E0%A6%A8%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%A6%E0%A6%AE%E0%A6%A0_(%E0%A7%A7%E0%A7%AF%E0%A7%A9%E0%A7%AE)%2F%E0%A6%AA%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%B0%E0%A6%A5%E0%A6%AE_%E0%A6%96%E0%A6%A3%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%A1%2F%E0%A6%A6%E0%A6%B6%E0%A6%AE_%E0%A6%AA%E0%A6%B0%E0%A6%BF%E0%A6%9A%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%9B%E0%A7%87%E0%A6%A6" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ISO 15919).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (10)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: ISO 15919 applied to the Bengali script as written, so ব is b throughout (bande, bidyā, dbisapta, tbaṁ, karabāle, baradāṁ); a Sanskrit-style reading of the same words would be vande, vidyā, dvisapta, tvaṁ, karavāle, varadāṁ. ং is ṁ, য় is ẏ, ড় is ṛ, ঋ-kar is r̥, danda | and double danda ||.</li><li>line 1.22: তোমারই is tomāra + i (the emphatic -i); ISO 15919's colon (tomāra:i) keeps it apart from the diphthong ai. Pronounced tomari.</li><li>line 1.3: malayaja, 'born of Malaya' (the Malabar hills of the south), usually the cool southern breeze; it can also mean sandalwood (cool with sandal paste).</li><li>line 1.10-1.11: seventy million (sapta koṭi), twice that many arms: the population of Bengal presidency as Bankim reckoned it. Later versions sing 'thirty crore' for all India; this text has seven.</li><li>line 1.12: disputed sense. abalā is 'without strength' and also a common word for 'woman'; eta bale can be 'with so much strength' (bal, strength) or '(why do they) call / say (you are)'. Read either 'why, Mother, are you powerless with so much strength?' or 'who says, Mother, you are weak?'.</li><li>line 1.14: tāriṇī, 'she who carries across' (to safety; a name of the goddess as saviour).</li><li>line 1.17-1.18, 1.20-1.23 are Bengali; the rest is Sanskrit. 'dharma' is kept untranslated (duty, right order, religion).</li><li>line 2.2-2.3: Kamalā is Lakshmi (goddess of fortune, seated on a lotus); Vāṇī is Sarasvatī (goddess of speech and learning).</li><li>line 2.10: the text has শ্যামালাং (śyāmālāṁ, with a long ā), where 1.4 has śyāmalāṁ; probably a slip of the edition for śyāmalāṁ 'dark green'. Romanized as written. saralāṁ: 'straight, simple, guileless'; 'upright' is a choice.</li><li>line 2.12: ধরনীং is written with dental ন (dharanīṁ) where the Sanskrit word is dharaṇī 'the earth'; romanized as written. bharaṇīm: 'she who supports, nourishes'.</li></ul></details></section>
